'''awsome,''' ''a.'' See ''awesome.''

'''ax<sup>1</sup>, axe<sup>1</sup>''' (aks), ''n.'' [The reg. mod. spelling is ''ax'', < ME. ''ax'', also ''axe'', ''ex'', ''æx'', < AS. ''æx'', also ''eax'', = ONorth. ''acasa'', ''acase'' = OS. ''accus'' = OD. ''akes'', D. ''aks'', ''aakse'', ''aaks'', = OHG. ''acchus'', ''achus'', MHG. ''ackes'', ''axt'', G. ''ax'', ''axt'' = Icel. ''öx'', ''öxi'' = Sw. ''yxa'' = Dan. ''öxe'' = L. ''ascia'', ax, mattock, akin to Gr. ἀξίνη, ax.] An instrument used for hewing timber and chopping wood, and also
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''A'', Broad-ax, for hewing; ''B'', Ax for chopping.}}}}
as a weapon of offense. {{smaller|The modern ax consists of a head of iron, with an arching edge of steel, and a}}

